00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.310
Let's start with what is Cloud
Armor, and how does it help.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.250
Cloud Armor it's
a network security

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.200
offering for protecting
your Google Cloud

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:43.110
deployments against
Layer 3 through Layer 7

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:46.170
DDoS attacks by leveraging
Google's global edge

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:49.020
infrastructure and
DDoS defense expertise.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.100
Cloud Armor works with global
HTTP/HTTPS load balancer,

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:56.520
which provides built-in
infrastructure DDoS defense.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:00:59.250
You can create rules using
combination of Layer 3

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:03.630
to 7 parameters and geolocation
to protect your deployment.

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:05.610
You can also use
predefined rules

00:01:05.610 --> 00:01:08.970
to defend against cross-site
scripting and SQL injection

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:10.080
defenses.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.130
Create the policy and apply
to more than one service.

00:01:14.130 --> 00:01:16.230
In Stackdriver
Logging, you can see

00:01:16.230 --> 00:01:18.330
if the policy was
matched and applied

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.310
to the incoming request.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.640
You don't always have to apply
the policy and rules in Access

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:24.660
or Deny mode.

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:26.550
You can understand
the access patterns

00:01:26.550 --> 00:01:29.050
by using Preview
mode to ensure you

00:01:29.050 --> 00:01:30.870
are blocking correct traffic.

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:32.850
You can also enforce
access control

00:01:32.850 --> 00:01:38.280
based on IPv4 and IPv6
addresses or CIDR blocks.

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:39.420
All right, enough talk.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:41.945
Now let's see how to
set up Cloud Armor.

00:01:41.945 --> 00:01:45.060
On Google Cloud console,
choose Network Security

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.710
under Networking, and
then Create Policy.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:53.610
Give your policy a name.

00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.520
Select the rule action
from Allow and Deny.

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:03.550
Then Add Rule.

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.350
Give your rule a description.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.900
Under Conditions, choose a type.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:09.400
Right now, under
the beta release,

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.770
you can select IP
address ranges.

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:15.070
Provide the matching criteria
with IP address range

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:19.360
and select the
action Allow or Deny.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:21.520
Initially, you may
want to just preview

00:02:21.520 --> 00:02:23.410
the traffic under this rule.

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:27.180
So select Enable
checkbox to just review.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:29.850
Priority determines the order
in which the rules are checked.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:34.090
So accordingly, we're just
going to set 1,000 in this case.

00:02:34.090 --> 00:02:36.700
Now, the last step
is to set up targets.

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:41.050
Targets are GCP resources that
you want to control access to.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.720
You will need to have a
predefined, load-balanced

00:02:43.720 --> 00:02:44.530
target.

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.860
So select that as one of
the targets that you want.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:51.010
You will see the policy
create on the dashboard.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:56.140
In order to test if the policy
worked, access the backend.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:03:09.880
In Google Cloud console,
navigate to Stackdriver Logging

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:11.560
and expand the recent log.

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.290
And you will see the
enforced security policy

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:16.360
under JSON Payload.

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:18.170
There you have it.

00:03:18.170 --> 00:03:21.640
You just created a simple
backless policy in Cloud Armor

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.190
and verified that it worked
in Stackdriver Logging.

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:27.404
So Mark, that's how easy it
is to set up Cloud Armor.

